course: dictionary
lesson: 2278
title: Creepy: strange, frightening, and uneasy
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Read the clues around a word to work out what it means. «Alice heads for the March Hare's house, with ear-shaped chimneys and a fur roof, makes herself two feet tall and walks up nervously: a strange, dreamy part, both funny and a bit creepy.» The clues are strange, dreamy and nervously, so creepy must mean strange and a little frightening. Check it by swapping in your meaning: a strange, dreamy part, both funny and a bit frightening. It still makes sense.
explain: Creepy can describe a place, a noise, or a person. A creepy house makes you uneasy. A creepy person makes you feel uncomfortable or unsafe because of how they act. To decide which one is meant, ask yourself: is the thing itself strange and scary, or is someone behaving in a way that worries you?
explain: Creepy is an informal word, so you will hear it in chatty talk and stories more than in formal writing. Creepy-crawly is a different sense of the same word: a small crawling thing, such as an insect or spider. The words around it tell you which meaning the writer wants.
fact: Creepy is an adjective, so it describes a noun, as in a creepy house.
fact: The comparative forms are creepier and creepiest.
fact: «Creepy» means strange and frightening in a way that makes you uneasy.
fact: «Creepy-crawly» means a small insect or spider.
fact: «Give me the creeps» means it makes you feel scared or uncomfortable.
exercise: «Alice walks up nervously: a strange, dreamy part, both funny and a bit creepy.» What does creepy mean here? | *Strange and a little frightening | Loud and busy | Very wet and cold | Kind and friendly
exercise: Ruby shouts, «There is a creepy-crawly in the bath!» What is a creepy-crawly? | A type of cake | A big, friendly dog | *A small insect or spider | A loud bell
exercise: Someone online seems creepy and asks you to keep a secret from your parents. What should you do? | *Tell a trusted adult straight away | Ignore them and say nothing | Send them your home address | Reply with a joke and keep chatting
test: Which word is the comparative form of creepy? | creeping | creepy-crawly | creepiness | *creepier
test: Which situation makes a person seem creepy? | A teacher who helps you find your book | A man who smiles, waves and says hello to the class | A friend who shares a sandwich | *A stranger who keeps staring at you and asks for your address
exam: What does «give me the creeps» mean? | It makes you feel warm and happy | It makes you feel very hungry | It makes you feel very tired | *It makes you feel scared or uncomfortable
